.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt SIGACTION 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sigaction ,
.Nm sigprocmask ,
.Nm sigsuspend ,
.Nm rt_sigaction ,
.Nm rt_sigprocmask ,
.Nm rt_sigsuspend ,
.Nm sigreturn ,
.Nm rt_sigreturn ,
.Nm pause
.Nd control signal dispositions and masks
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In signal.h
.Ft int
.Fn sigaction "int sig" "const struct sigaction *restrict act" "struct sigaction *restrict oldact"
.Ft int
.Fn sigprocmask "int how" "const sigset_t *restrict set" "sigset_t *restrict oldset"
.Ft int
.Fn sigsuspend "const sigset_t *mask"
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn pause void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn sigaction
installs the disposition in
.Fa act
for signal
.Fa sig .
If
.Fa oldact
is non-null, it receives the previous disposition.
A null
.Fa act
leaves the disposition unchanged.
The kernel rejects even a query for
.Dv SIGKILL
or
.Dv SIGSTOP ;
neither can be caught, ignored, or blocked.
.Pp
The kernel accepts signal numbers 1 through 64.
Numbers 1 through 31 have their Linux i386 meanings.
Musl reserves numbers 32 through 34 and rejects them in
.Fn sigaction .
Pending signals occupy one bit each; repeated instances coalesce.
There is no queue of real-time signals or sender information.
.Ss Dispositions
.Va sa_handler
selects
.Dv SIG_DFL ,
.Dv SIG_IGN ,
or a handler address.
Installing
.Dv SIG_IGN
also discards an instance already pending.
The default actions are:
.Bl -tag -width "Terminate"
.It Ignore
.Dv SIGCHLD , SIGURG , SIGWINCH ,
and
.Dv SIGCONT .
.It Stop
.Dv SIGSTOP , SIGTSTP , SIGTTIN ,
and
.Dv SIGTTOU .
.It Terminate
Every other accepted signal number.
.El
.Pp
Posting
.Dv SIGCONT
resumes a stopped process and removes pending stop signals, regardless
of its disposition or blocked status.
A stop signal removes pending
.Dv SIGCONT .
.Dv SIGKILL
makes a stopped process runnable so it can terminate.
Stop and continue events notify the parent with
.Dv SIGCHLD
and are reported by
.Xr wait 2 .
The booted init process cannot be stopped.
.Pp
Signals act on return to user mode from a system call or interrupt.
The lowest-numbered pending, unblocked signal is selected first.
An ignored signal does not interrupt a blocking wait.
A caught, stopping, or terminating signal can interrupt a wait;
an operation that has transferred data may return a partial count.
.Pp
Unhandled user-mode CPU exceptions are different: they terminate the
process directly
without consulting its signal mask or disposition.
A handler for a fault signal can run when that signal is posted,
but cannot recover from the corresponding CPU exception.
.Ss Handlers
While a handler runs, its
.Va sa_mask
is added to the blocked mask.
The delivered signal is also blocked unless
.Dv SA_NODEFER
is set.
.Dv SIGKILL
and
.Dv SIGSTOP
are removed from this mask.
.Dv SA_RESETHAND
resets the disposition to default before entering the handler.
.Pp
With
.Dv SA_SIGINFO ,
the handler receives pointers to signal information and context storage.
Only
.Va si_signo
is filled; the remaining information and context storage are zero.
They do not contain a complete Linux signal context.
.Pp
All action flags are stored and returned.
.Dv SA_RESTART
does not restart interrupted system calls.
.Dv SA_ONSTACK , SA_NOCLDSTOP ,
and
.Dv SA_NOCLDWAIT
have no special effect.
.Ss Masks and waiting
.Fn sigprocmask
returns the previous mask through a non-null
.Fa oldset .
If
.Fa set
is non-null,
.Fa how
selects
.Dv SIG_BLOCK
(0) to add its signals,
.Dv SIG_UNBLOCK
(1) to remove them, or
.Dv SIG_SETMASK
(2) to replace the mask.
With a null
.Fa set ,
the mask is unchanged and
.Fa how
is ignored.
.Pp
.Fn pause
waits for a signal that can act.
.Fn sigsuspend
installs
.Fa mask
for the wait and restores the previous mask before returning.
Unlike Linux, the kernel restores that mask before delivering the signal.
If the old mask blocks the signal that ended the wait, the signal remains
pending; otherwise the handler runs with the restored mask, modified by
the handler's own masking rules.
.Ss System call interface
Musl's
.Vt sigset_t
occupies 128 bytes.
The kernel uses an 8-byte set: two 32-bit words for signals 1 through 32
and 33 through 64, with bit
.Li sig-1
representing a signal.
Musl passes this smaller size to the kernel.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers and register arguments are:
.Bl -tag -width "rt_sigprocmask (175)"
.It Nm rt_sigaction No (174)
EBX is the signal, ECX the new action pointer, EDX the old action pointer,
and ESI the set size, which must be 8.
The kernel action is five 32-bit words: handler address, flags, restorer
address, and the two mask words.
.Fn sigaction
converts between this layout and the public structure.
.It Nm rt_sigprocmask No (175)
EBX is
.Fa how ,
ECX the new set pointer, EDX the old set pointer, and ESI the set size, 8.
Musl's
.Fn sigprocmask
uses this call.
.It Nm rt_sigsuspend No (179)
EBX points to the temporary set and ECX is its size, 8.
Musl's
.Fn sigsuspend
uses this call.
.It Nm pause No (29)
No arguments.
.It Nm sigreturn No (119)
Musl's plain-handler restorer pops the signal argument before issuing
the call.
The saved frame begins 8 bytes below the user stack pointer.
.It Nm rt_sigreturn No (173)
Musl's
.Dv SA_SIGINFO
restorer issues the call without popping the signal argument.
The saved frame begins 4 bytes below the user stack pointer.
.El
.Pp
Musl declares no public functions for the
.Li rt_*
calls or
.Nm sigreturn .
It supplies the restorer address when installing an action.
The kernel always uses that address; it supplies no fallback restorer.
.Pp
Both return calls validate the same kernel-built frame and restore the
saved registers, instruction and stack pointers, floating-point state,
and pre-handler signal mask.
Segment selectors and processor flags are restricted to safe user values.
An unreadable or invalid frame, including an invalid floating-point
image, terminates the process with
.Dv SIGSEGV .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn sigaction
and
.Fn sigprocmask
return 0 on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
.Fn pause
and
.Fn sigsuspend
return -1 with
.Va errno
set to
.Er EINTR
when the wait ends and execution continues.
The kernel calls return negative error numbers.
The signal-return calls resume saved execution, including its EAX value.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/sigprobe.c
installs a
.Dv SIGUSR1
handler, blocks and posts the signal, then unblocks it and checks that
the handler runs and returns through
.Nm rt_sigreturn .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
An action or signal-set pointer cannot be read or written as required.
.It Bq Er EINTR
The wait in
.Fn pause
or
.Fn sigsuspend
ended because a signal could act.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The signal is outside 1 through 64, is
.Dv SIGKILL
or
.Dv SIGSTOP ,
or is reserved by musl;
a kernel set size is not 8; or
.Fa how
is invalid when a new mask is supplied.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
